PLD laydown harness
===================

One job: take an off-set garment photo and get it to lie like a reference
laydown - square, flat, wrinkles relaxed, clean white plate, no shadows -
without redrawing the garment.

    ./run.sh --yolo                     # reference chosen from the library

    ./run.sh --source inputs/off_set_image.jpg \
             --reference inputs/reference_greyscale.jpg \
             --max-images 6 --yolo      # ...or supplied, which skips the search

    ./run.sh --max-images 0 --yolo      # runs the whole loop, bills nothing


How it works
------------

A model with a shell and eight purpose-built tools iterates towards the
reference. Turn 1 it is handed both images inline - the segmented source and the
reference - and from then on it chooses freely:

  * look at an image, or compare two in a single vision call
  * write and surgically edit the prompt, one named section at a time
  * generate, from the original source OR from any candidate it already made
  * segment a CANDIDATE to clean up a plate it does not like
  * note what it concluded, mark its best, finish

There is no fixed order and no pipeline. Segmenting a generated image and then
generating again from it is a legitimate move, and so is throwing the prompt away
on turn nine.

Every candidate is pushed back into the conversation as a picture with three
free measurements printed underneath, so the model never has to remember to ask.


The budget
----------

fal.ai images are capped for the whole run - 10 by default, from
LAYDOWN_MAX_IMAGES, lowered per run with --max-images. The counter never
refills. Everything else is free and unlimited: looking, comparing, measuring,
segmenting, rewriting the prompt.

--max-images 0 is supported and useful. The loop runs, generate refuses, nothing
is billed, and the run exits 0. It is how you check a deployment for free.


The three numbers
-----------------

    tools/metrics.py --run runs/<stamp>

Silhouette IoU, colour dE76, and wrinkle energy as a candidate/source ratio -
all against the ORIGINAL source, never a candidate's parent. No model, no
network, no money.

They are advice, never a gate. Nothing is blocked, scored or ranked by them.
They exist for the one failure eyes reliably miss: the generator drawing a NEW
garment that is already flat instead of re-laying the real one. task/SKILL.md
carries the measured bands - read those before reading a number, because the
useful ranges are not the obvious ones (a normal candidate's wrinkle ratio is
ABOVE 1, not below).


What a run leaves behind
------------------------

    runs/<stamp>/
      run.log             the full trace - reasoning, every message, every tool
      transcript.jsonl    structured turns
      steps.log           the run in a few lines, including the spend
      LOG.md              the model's own write-up
      reference_selection.json  which library image won, what it scored, and
                                what was disqualified. Written whether or not
                                anything matched, so `match_found` is always
                                there to branch on.
      reference_match.jpg the garment beside the top candidates and their scores
      archive/
        source_clean.jpg  the segmented source - what fal actually receives
        reference_greyscale.jpg   the reference, desaturated - image 2
        reference_original.jpg    the library asset, colour, untouched
        prompt_sections.json    the editable prompt
        prompt_<hash>.txt       a snapshot per generation
        cand_01.png ...         every attempt
        cand_03s.png            a candidate after segmentation
        lineage.json      parent, depth, prompt hash and seed per candidate
        notes.json        the model's own note per candidate
        uploads.json      content hash -> fal URL, so nothing uploads twice
      output/
        best.png          the delivery


The reference
-------------

Chosen from inputs/reference_library, or supplied with --reference. Passing the
flag skips the search entirely; its absence is what triggers one.

It is desaturated on the way into the run folder. That is deliberate and it is
the one thing done TO it: it is a shape and lay reference, never a colour
target, and sent in colour it gets read as one - the garment comes back wearing
the reference's tone.

    tools/select_reference.py --index       describe the library, once
    tools/select_reference.py --calibrate   where to put the threshold
    tools/select_reference.py --dry-run     decide, install nothing

Step 0 does it in three stages, none of them billed. It DESCRIBES the garment
and every library image against one fixed form - body region, sleeve, leg,
length, silhouette, front opening, neckline, waist, hem, fabric, structure. It
SCORES each one out of 100 in plain arithmetic, disqualifying outright anything
whose lay could not transfer at all: a different body region, sleeves against
none, legs against none, a front that opens all the way against one that does
not. Then it CONFIRMS, showing the survivors to the model, which picks one or
answers "none".

Both gates must pass - the score clears the threshold AND the model does not
reject the pick. Otherwise the run stops at step 0 and exits 20, having
generated nothing. runs/<stamp>/reference_match.jpg shows the near misses.

Descriptions are cached on content in .cache/refmatch, so the library is
described once and every run after that is free. Run --index after adding to it.

The threshold is PROVISIONAL. 78 was measured on nine of this project's own
assets, not on your library - run --calibrate, which scores every library image
against every other and prints where same-garment pairs sit against
different-garment ones, and pass what you read off it as --reference-threshold.

Whatever the reference turns out to be, it is a different product. The model is
asked what still differs between it and the garment, and that sentence is
scanned for words naming CONSTRUCTION rather than pose - seam, placket, pocket,
zip. Any hit goes into the agent's opening brief, because a seam copied off the
reference looks exactly like one the garment always had.


Chained edits
-------------

generate(source="cand_03") starts from an earlier attempt instead of the
original. It is how one remaining defect gets fixed without losing what already
worked, and it compounds drift: image 1 is then a generated image, so anything
invented on the first pass arrives on the second labelled as the real product.

lineage.json records the depth and it is printed with each candidate. Two edits
deep is where invented detail starts to harden. Segmenting a candidate does NOT
add depth - it drops a background and cannot invent anything.


Services it talks to
--------------------

    QWEN_BASE_URL       the model, OpenAI-compatible; a /v1 suffix is stripped
                        default http://10.11.245.41:8091
    QWEN_API_KEY        or .qwen_key on disk
    QWEN_MODEL          which model to route on. Optional against a server
                        holding one - the harness reads /v1/models and takes
                        what is there. Required against a proxy (LiteLLM),
                        which fronts several and rejects an unnamed request.
    SEGMENT_URL         the SAM3 segmentation service
                        default http://10.11.245.145:4000/sam3-segment
    SEGMENT_API_KEY     its key, which is NOT the model's. Leave unset for a
                        service that wants no auth, such as the box itself at
                        http://10.11.245.41:8780/segment
    FAL_KEY             from .env; bills real money

Segmentation drops the BACKGROUND only. A hang tag, ticket, pin or clip attached
to the garment survives into the source - which is why the prompt guardrails
warn about naming them rather than refusing, and why the skill says to look
before you decide.


Reading a run
-------------

run.log is the only place three things exist: the model's chain of thought, every
message exactly as it was sent, and the output of the steps that ran before the
first turn. Format is

    timestamp | elapsed | LEVEL | component | message

with multi-line payloads indented under a | and components named http, turn,
reasoning, tool, bash, vision, approval, context, step0, session, console.


old/
----

The retired reference-matching and grading pipeline - 14 tools, the 45-image
library, the layout profiles and the previous skill. Nothing imports it. See
old/README.md for what each piece did and why it went.


Container
---------

See DOCKER.md. Two inputs, one output, and the exit-code contract - including
the finish-status table and why exit 20 can no longer occur.
